From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 11:45:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] swap-on: give zram priority over disk swap

swap-on.sh enabled zram without a priority. The kernel then hands out -2, -3, ... in the order swap
areas are activated, so a swap partition enabled before it (the store-swap LVM volume on LuneOS
devices) ranked higher and was filled first: on the HP TouchPad 82 MB of the UI went to eMMC swap
while the 350 MB zram disk stayed empty, and the tablet stalled on swap-in.

Enable zram at priority 100, above any partition swap.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/common/lib/systemd/system/scripts/swap-on.sh.in	2026-10-07 11:30:08.506132508 +0200
+++ b/common/lib/systemd/system/scripts/swap-on.sh.in	2026-10-07 11:30:19.416327218 +0200
@@ -22,7 +22,10 @@
 
 mkswap $SWAP_DEV > /dev/null 2>&1 || true;
 
-if swapon --discard $SWAP_DEV; then
+# zram before any disk swap: swapon with no priority gives -2, -3, ... in order of activation,
+# so a swap partition enabled earlier (a LuneOS device's store-swap volume) was filled first and
+# zram sat empty while pages went to eMMC.
+if swapon --discard -p 100 $SWAP_DEV; then
     echo "Swapon succeeded"
 else
     echo "Swapon failed"
